# TLS policy — the CLOUD copy. Mounted beside the Caddyfile as `mode.caddy`.
#
# Every site block in the Caddyfile imports one of these three snippets instead
# of writing a `tls` directive inline. That indirection is the entire mechanism
# that lets one routing table serve a cluster with a public IP and a workstation
# cluster without one: the routing is identical, only this file differs.
#
# k8s/local/mode.caddy is the other copy. It is eight lines and defines all
# three as no-ops.

# The default for sparx-owned hostnames and for the tenant catch-all. Issues a
# certificate per hostname on first HTTPS request, gated by the ask endpoint in
# the Caddyfile's global block.
#
# Per-hostname issuance is why nested tenant hosts work here and did not behind
# the tunnel: `site.tenant.sparx.zone` gets its own certificate, so the one-label
# matching limit of a wildcard certificate (RFC 6125 §6.4.3) never applies.
(tls_policy) {
	tls {
		on_demand
	}
}

# For hostnames the ask endpoint does not know — kanninja.com and friends, which
# are not sparx tenants and would be REJECTED by /internal/domain-check. An
# empty snippet leaves Caddy's automatic HTTPS in charge, which mints a normal
# managed Let's Encrypt certificate at startup via HTTP-01 / TLS-ALPN.
(tls_managed) {
}

# admin.wize.works only. A Cloudflare Origin CA certificate, mounted from the
# caddy-admin-origin secret by the bootstrap workflow. Not ACME: Cloudflare
# Access sits in front of this host and intercepts /.well-known/acme-challenge/*
# with its own login page, so no ACME challenge can ever complete while the
# record is proxied.
(tls_admin_origin) {
	tls /certs/admin/tls.crt /certs/admin/tls.key
}

# Plain HTTP is only ever a redirect to HTTPS. Lives here rather than in the
# Caddyfile because in workstation mode :80 is where the tenant catch-all
# listens — the two would collide.
:80 {
	redir https://{host}{uri} permanent
}
